# Installed by the dictate-uinput package, which exists for this one rule:
# installing it is the admin's choice to let the user at the seat write to
# /dev/uinput. Only the paste and type methods of dictate need it. The grant
# is the seat's: a virtual keyboard opened under it keeps working after a
# user switch. Ubuntu's steam-devices makes the same grant, the same way.
#
# ydotool synthesises keystrokes by writing to /dev/uinput, for the paste and type
# methods of dictate. The ydotool package
# ships 80-uinput.rules, which makes that node root:input 0660, so reaching it
# means being in the `input` group -- and supplementary groups are only read at
# login, so the run that grants the group cannot make it effective.
#
# uaccess instead has logind put an ACL on the node for whoever is logged in at
# the seat. It applies immediately and again on every login, so dictation works
# on the run that installs it and after a reboot, with no logout in between.
#
# The number is the whole point. /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/73-seat-late.rules is
# what runs the uaccess builtin, on `TAG=="uaccess"`, so the tag has to be set
# before 73 is reached. A tag first set after it is invisible to that match in
# the same event and only shows up in a later one, which reads as working for
# as long as a previous event left the tag in the database.
#
# It is not about 80-uinput.rules, which only sets MODE and GROUP and does not
# interact with the tag at all.
# static_node: where uinput is a module, /dev/uinput exists before it is
# loaded, and logind gives that node the ACL at login too. Built in, as on
# Ubuntu's kernels, the option is a no-op.
ACTION!="remove", KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", TAG+="uaccess", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput"
